# `Type=forking` acceptance unit (plan §7.10).
#
# This is the one unit in the tree that exists to be a *bad* example: a daemon
# that detaches instead of running in the foreground. rinit starts it, waits for
# the main process to exit, and then has to work out which process it left
# behind - which is what `PIDFile=` is for, and why the plan says new services
# should foreground (`simple`/`notify`) and this type is for legacy ones.
#
# The shell backgrounds a `sleep` and exits 0 immediately, which is the shape of
# a double-fork daemon: the `sleep` is reparented to PID 1, it is *not* the
# leader of its process group (so a group signal would not reach it - rinit
# falls back to the pid), and the `PIDFile=` is the only thing that says which
# process the unit now is.
#
# It is started on demand (`rctl start forking.service`) like the other demo
# units, so the console drives it and the assertions have a known start. There is
# no `Cgroup*=` on purpose: with a `PIDFile=` the cgroup is not needed to find
# the daemon, and this keeps the file about the lookup rather than about limits.
[Unit]
Description=Type=forking demo (started on demand)

[Service]
Type=forking
PIDFile=/run/forking.pid
ExecStart=/bin/sh -c "/bin/sleep 300 >/dev/null 2>&1 & echo $! > /run/forking.pid; exit 0"
